module agentic

# ========================================
# ENVIRONMENT TYPE (Organization-scoped, Personal Resource)
# ========================================
# Environment represents a named collection of configuration and secrets.
# Every environment belongs to an organization. Contains encrypted secrets.
#
# Authorization model (PERSONAL RESOURCE with opt-in org sharing):
# - PRIVATE (default): only owner (creator) and explicitly granted users
#   can view. Org admins are explicitly EXCLUDED (personal secrets).
# - ORG (visibility_org, opt-in): all org members can view — with secret
#   values REDACTED — via the explicit org viewer tuple written by
#   VisibilityTupleReconciler. Setting ORG also marks the environment as
#   runtime-resolvable for executions in the owning org (see below).
# - PUBLIC/PLATFORM are not supported for environments (kind
#   VisibilityConfig: supports_org only) — secret values must never be
#   resolvable across the org boundary.
# - Personal (stigmer.ai/personal) and OAuth-managed (stigmer.ai/managed)
#   environments reject org visibility entirely (handler-level guardrail).
#
# Secret access is TWO DISTINCT OPERATIONS with distinct authorization:
#
# 1. HUMAN REVEAL (getSecretValue RPC) — creator-only via can_read_secrets,
#    at every visibility level. Viewers (explicit grants AND org members on
#    shared environments) always see redacted values. This is the
#    GitHub-org-secrets model: write-only for humans, usable by the runtime.
#
# 2. RUNTIME RESOLUTION (execution-context creation) — NOT modeled in FGA.
#    The execution engine resolves an org-shared environment's secrets
#    server-side (EnvironmentRuntimeResolutionService) under one app-level
#    policy: metadata.visibility == org AND environment org == execution
#    org. Private environments resolve as-caller (creator-only), unchanged.
#    This is deliberately app-level, mirroring the sharing gates (decided
#    2026-07-09 and 2026-07-12): no per-share tuples, and no FGA relation
#    could allow an org member's *execution* to use a secret while denying
#    the same member's *reveal*.
#
# FGA tuples on creation:
# - environment:my-env#organization@organization:acme
# - environment:my-env#owner@identity_account:alice
# - environment:my-env#creator@identity_account:alice
#
# Additional tuple when visibility_org is set (removed on revert to private):
# - environment:my-env#viewer@organization:acme#viewer
#
# CHANNEL CREDENTIALS (documented deviation): the managed environment that
# holds an agent channel's provider credential (e.g. the Slack bot token
# the Slack install flow stores) carries a SECOND owner and creator tuple
# for the org's system-channel identity account, written at install time by
# ChannelIdentityAccountService.grantCredentialsEnvironmentAccess. This
# deliberately bends the single-immutable-creator attribution note above:
# the party who stored the secret is the channel runtime (the token comes
# from the provider's OAuth exchange, not from the human), and the async
# webhook/delivery paths — which have no caller JWT — read it OnBehalfOf
# that account through the standard getSecretValue pipeline. The co-owner
# tuple additionally makes cross-owner re-install and the channel delete
# cascade pass can_edit/can_delete regardless of which human clicked. The
# installing human remains creator too and can reveal the token (accepted:
# installers hold can_edit on the channel itself). Scope: ONLY these
# system-managed channel-credential environments; nothing else may write a
# second creator tuple.

type environment
  relations
    # ========================================
    # ORGANIZATION OWNERSHIP
    # ========================================
    define organization: [organization]
    
    # ========================================
    # CREATOR (Immutable Attribution)
    # ========================================
    # The identity_account that originally created this resource.
    # Written once at creation, never changed. Distinct from owner:
    # owner = who can manage (mutable)
    # creator = who originally stored the secrets (immutable)
    define creator: [identity_account]
    
    # ========================================
    # OWNERSHIP (Personal Resource)
    # ========================================
    # Org admins explicitly excluded — environments are personal resources
    # containing secrets that admins have no business accessing.
    define owner: [identity_account]
    
    # ========================================
    # VIEWER ACCESS (Explicit Grants + Org Sharing)
    # ========================================
    # organization#viewer is the shape VisibilityTupleReconciler writes for
    # visibility_org (the org's full read audience — every role satisfies it);
    # organization#member is the legacy pre-2026-08-11 shape, kept resolving
    # until the boot backfill converges stored tuples. Viewers see REDACTED
    # secret values.
    define viewer: [identity_account, organization#member, organization#viewer] or owner
    
    # ========================================
    # PERMISSIONS
    # ========================================
    define can_view: viewer
    define can_edit: owner
    define can_delete: owner
    
    # ========================================
    # SECRET RETRIEVAL (Creator-Only, Every Visibility Level)
    # ========================================
    # Only the creator can read unredacted secret values — org sharing never
    # widens this. The execution engine resolves org-shared secrets through
    # the server-side runtime-resolution path (see type comment), not
    # through this permission.
    define can_read_secrets: creator
    
    # ========================================
    # IAM POLICY MANAGEMENT
    # ========================================
    define can_grant_access: owner
    define can_view_access: viewer
